NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:06.300
Estimating the bias and variance of your learning algorithm really helps you prioritize what to work on next.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:14.300
But the way you analyze bias and variance changes when your training set comes from a different distribution than your depth and test set.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:15.300
Let's see how.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:22.300
Let's keep using our CAD classification example and let's say humans get near perfect performance on this.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:28.300
So Bayes' error or Bayes' optimal error we know is nearly 0% on this problem.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:36.300
So to carry out error analysis, you usually look at the training error and also look at the error on the depth set.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:45.300
So let's say in this example that your training error is 1% and your depth error is 10%.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:49.300
If your depth data came from the same distribution as your training set,

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:56.300
you would say that here you have a large variance problem that your algorithm is just not generalizing well from the training set,

00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:01.300
which is doing well on to the depth set, which is suddenly doing much worse on.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:06.300
But in the setting where your training data and your depth data comes from different distribution,

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:09.300
you can no longer safely draw this conclusion.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:13.300
In particular, maybe it's doing just fine on the depth set.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:19.300
It's just that the training set was really easy because it was high res, very clear images,

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:23.300
and maybe the depth set is just much harder.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:33.300
So maybe there isn't a variance problem, and this just reflects that the depth set contains images that are much more difficult to classify accurately.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:39.300
So the problem with this analysis is that when you went from the training error to the depth error,

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.300
two things change at a time.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:46.300
One is that the algorithm saw data in the training set but not in the depth set.

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:50.300
Two, the distribution of data in your depth set is different.

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:56.300
And because you change two things at the same time, it's difficult to know of this 9% increase in error,

00:01:56.300 --> 00:02:01.300
how much of it is because the algorithm didn't see the data in the depth set.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:04.300
So that's sort of the variance part of the problem.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:08.300
And how much of it is because the depth set data is just different.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:13.300
So in order to tease out these two effects,

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:19.300
and if you didn't totally follow what these two different effects are, don't worry, we'll go over it again in a second.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:26.300
But in order to tease out these two effects, it will be useful to define a new piece of data which we call the training depth set.

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:33.300
So this is a new subset of data which you carve out that should have the same distribution as training sets,

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:37.300
but you don't explicitly train your neural network on this.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:39.300
So here's what I mean.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:50.300
Previously, we had set up some training set and some depth set and some test set as follows.

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:56.300
And the dev and test sets have the same distribution, but the training sets will have some different distribution.

00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:08.300
What we're going to do is randomly shuffle the training set and then carve out just a piece of the training set to be the training-depth set.

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:12.300
So just as the dev and test set have the same distribution,

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:18.300
the training set and the training-depth set also have the same distribution.

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:26.970
But the difference is that now you train your neural network just on the training set proper.

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:33.970
You won't let the neural network, you won't run back propagation on the training-depth portion of this data.

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:39.970
To carry out error analysis, what you should do is now look at the error of the classifier on the training set,

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:43.970
on the training-depth set, as well as on the dev set.

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:52.630
So let's say in this example that your training error is 1%,

00:03:52.630 --> 00:04:00.630
and let's say the error on the training-depth set is 9%,

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:08.630
and the error on the dev set is 10%, same as before.

00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:15.630
What you can conclude from this is that when you went from training data to training-depth data,

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:17.630
the error really went up a lot.

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:26.630
And the only difference between the training data and the training-depth data is that your neural network got to solve the first part of this.

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:33.630
It was trained explicitly on this, but it wasn't trained explicitly on the training-depth data.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:38.630
So this tells you that you have a variance problem,

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:45.630
because the training-depth error was measured on data that comes from the same distribution as your training set.

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:49.630
So you know that even though your neural network does well on the training set,

00:04:49.630 --> 00:04:54.630
your training set is just not generalizing well to data in the training-depth set,

00:04:54.630 --> 00:05:02.630
which comes from the same distribution, but it's just not generalizing well to data from the same distribution that it hadn't seen before.

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:07.630
So in this example, you have really a variance problem.

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:11.200
Let's look at a different example.

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:17.200
Let's say the training error is 1%, and the training-depth error is 1.5%,

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:21.200
but when you go to the depth set, your error is 10%.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:25.200
So now you have actually a pretty low variance problem,

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:32.200
because when you went from training data that you've seen to the training-depth data that the neural network has not seen,

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:37.200
the error increases only a little bit, but then it really jumps when you go to the depth set.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:40.200
So this is a data mismatch problem.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:44.200
Write data mismatch.

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:51.200
So this is a data mismatch problem,

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:57.200
because your learning algorithm was not trained explicitly on data from training-depth or depth,

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:00.200
but these two data sets come from different distributions.

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:05.200
But whatever your algorithm is learning, it works great on training-depth, but it doesn't work well on depth.

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:12.200
So somehow your algorithm has learned to do well on a different distribution than what you really care about.

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:17.660
So we call it a data mismatch problem.

00:06:17.660 --> 00:06:19.660
Let's just look at a few more examples.

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:24.660
I'll write this on the next row since I'm running out of space on top.

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:33.520
So training error, training-depth error, and depth error.

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:41.520
Let's say that training error is 10%, training-depth error is 11%, and depth error is 12%.

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:49.520
Remember that human level, proxy for Bayes' error, is roughly 0%.

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:57.520
So if you have this type of performance, then you really have a bias or an avoidable bias problem,

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:01.520
because you're doing much worse than human level.

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:05.520
So this is really a high bias setting.

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:13.520
And one last example, if your training error is 10%, your training-depth error is 11%,

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:19.520
and your depth error is 20%, then it looks like this actually has two issues.

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:26.520
One, the avoidable bias is quite high because you're not even doing that well on the training set.

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:31.520
Humans get nearly 0% error, but you're getting 10% error on the training set.

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:42.980
The variance here seems quite small, but this data mismatch is quite large.

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:48.980
So for this example, I would say you have a large bias or avoidable bias problem,

00:07:48.980 --> 00:07:56.380
as well as a data mismatch problem.

00:07:56.380 --> 00:08:02.380
So let's take what we've done on this slide and write out the general principles.

00:08:02.380 --> 00:08:11.380
The key quantities I would look at are human level error, your training set error,

00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:23.220
your training depth set error, so that same distribution is a training set,

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:28.220
but you didn't train explicitly on it, your depth set error.

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:31.220
And depending on the differences between these errors,

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:38.220
you can get a sense of how big is the avoidable bias, the variance, the data mismatch problems.

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:43.220
So let's say that human level error is 4%, your training error is 7%,

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:49.220
your training depth error is 10%, and your depth error is 12%.

00:08:49.220 --> 00:08:54.220
So this gives you a sense of the avoidable bias,

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:57.220
because you know you'd like your algorithm to do at least as well,

00:08:57.220 --> 00:09:00.220
or approach human level performance maybe on the training set.

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:03.220
This is a sense of the variance.

00:09:03.220 --> 00:09:09.220
So how well do you generalize from the training set to the training depth set?

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:14.220
This is a sense of how much of a data mismatch problem you have.

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:17.220
And technically, you could also add one more thing,

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:19.220
which is the test set performance.

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:20.220
And we write test error.

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:23.220
You shouldn't be doing development on your test set,

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:25.220
because you don't want to overfit your test set.

00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:28.220
But if you also look at this, then this gap here

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:36.300
tells you the degree of overfitting to the depth set.

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:40.770
So there's a huge gap between your depth set performance

00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:41.770
and your test set performance.

00:09:41.770 --> 00:09:44.770
It means you may be overtuned to the depth set.

00:09:44.770 --> 00:09:48.770
And so maybe you need to find a bigger depth set.

00:09:48.770 --> 00:09:52.770
So remember that your depth set and your test set come from the same distribution.

00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:54.770
So the only way for there to be a huge gap here,

00:09:54.770 --> 00:09:57.770
for it to do much better on the depth set and the test set,

00:09:57.770 --> 00:10:00.770
is if you somehow manage to overfit the depth set.

00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:03.770
And if that's the case, what you might consider doing is going back

00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:05.770
and just getting more depth set data.

00:10:05.770 --> 00:10:09.770
Now, I've written these numbers as if as you go down the list,

00:10:09.770 --> 00:10:12.770
the numbers always keep going up.

00:10:12.770 --> 00:10:16.770
Here's one example of numbers that doesn't always go up.

00:10:16.770 --> 00:10:21.770
Maybe human level performance is 4%, training error is 7%,

00:10:21.770 --> 00:10:23.770
training depth error is 10%.

00:10:23.770 --> 00:10:25.770
But let's say that when you go to the depth set,

00:10:25.770 --> 00:10:29.770
you find that you actually surprisingly do much better on the depth set.

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:32.770
Maybe this is 6%, 6% as well.

00:10:32.770 --> 00:10:41.420
So I've seen effects like this working on, for example,

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:45.420
a speech recognition task where the training data turned out to be

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:48.420
much harder than your depth set and test set.

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:52.420
So these two were evaluated on your training set distribution,

00:10:52.420 --> 00:10:56.420
and these two were evaluated on your depth test set distribution.

00:10:56.420 --> 00:11:01.420
So sometimes if your depth test set distribution is much easier

00:11:01.420 --> 00:11:03.420
for whatever application you're working on,

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:06.420
then these numbers can actually go down.

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:08.420
So if you see funny things like this,

00:11:08.420 --> 00:11:11.420
there's an even more general formulation of this analysis

00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:12.420
that might be helpful.

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:15.420
Let me quickly explain that on the next slide.

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:21.960
So let me motivate this using the speech activated

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:25.960
rear view mirror example.

00:11:25.960 --> 00:11:28.960
It turns out that the numbers we've been writing down

00:11:28.960 --> 00:11:33.960
can be placed into a table where on the horizontal axis

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:35.960
I'm going to place different data sets.

00:11:35.960 --> 00:11:42.960
So for example, you might have data from your general speech recognition tasks.

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:46.960
So you might have a bunch of data that you've just collected

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:49.960
from a lot of speech recognition problems you've worked on,

00:11:49.960 --> 00:11:52.960
from smart speakers, data you purchase, and so on.

00:11:52.960 --> 00:12:00.960
And then you also have the rear view mirror specific speech data

