which is how well do humans do on this category of data,

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:05.500
say data from all sorts of speech recognition tasks,

00:13:05.500 --> 00:13:09.500
you know, the 5,000 utterances that you could power into your training set.

00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:12.500
And an example on the previous slide, this is 4%.

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:20.500
This number here was our, maybe the training error,

00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:26.260
which in the example on the previous slide was 7%.

00:13:26.260 --> 00:13:33.620
If your learning algorithm has seen this example, performed gradient descent as an example,

00:13:33.620 --> 00:13:37.620
and this example came from your training set distribution

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:39.620
or some general speech recognition distribution,

00:13:39.620 --> 00:13:43.620
how well does your algorithm do on the example it has trained on?

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:53.000
Then here is the training depth set error.

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:57.000
It's usually a bit higher, which is for data from this distribution,

00:13:57.000 --> 00:14:02.000
from general speech recognition, if your algorithm did not train explicitly

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:06.000
on some examples from this distribution, how well does it do?

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:10.340
And that's what we call the training depth error.

00:14:10.340 --> 00:14:17.340
And then if you move over to the right, this box here is the depth set error

00:14:17.340 --> 00:14:24.970
or maybe also the test set error, which was 6% in the example just now.

00:14:24.970 --> 00:14:27.970
And depth and test set error are actually technically two numbers,

00:14:27.970 --> 00:14:31.970
but either one could go into this box here.

00:14:31.970 --> 00:14:34.970
And this is if you have data from the rearview mirror,

00:14:34.970 --> 00:14:38.970
from actually recorded in a car from the rearview mirror application,

00:14:38.970 --> 00:14:43.970
but your neural network did not perform back propagation on this example.

00:14:43.970 --> 00:14:45.970
What is the error?

