mirror speech data and just measure how good humans are at this task.

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:37.970
And maybe this turns out also to be 6%, and the way you do that is you take some

00:15:37.970 --> 00:15:40.970
rearview mirror speech data, put in the training set so the neural network

00:15:40.970 --> 00:15:45.970
learns on it as well, and then you measure the error on that subset of the data.

00:15:45.970 --> 00:15:48.970
But if this is what you get, then well, it turns out that you're actually

00:15:48.970 --> 00:15:53.970
already performing at the level of humans on this rearview mirror speech data.

00:15:53.970 --> 00:15:57.970
So maybe you're actually doing quite well on that distribution of data.

00:15:57.970 --> 00:16:02.970
When you do this most of skidded analysis, it doesn't always give you a one clear

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:06.970
powerful width, but sometimes it just gives you additional insights as well.

00:16:06.970 --> 00:16:10.970
So for example, comparing these two numbers in this case tells us that for

00:16:10.970 --> 00:16:15.970
humans, the rearview mirror speech data is actually harder than for general

