speech recognition because humans get 6% error rather than 4% error.

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:26.970
But then looking at these differences as well may help you understand bias

00:16:26.970 --> 00:16:29.970
and variance and data mismatch problems to different degrees.

00:16:29.970 --> 00:16:34.970
So this more general formulation is something I've used a few times.

00:16:34.970 --> 00:16:39.970
I've not used it, but for a lot of problems, you find that examining

00:16:39.970 --> 00:16:44.970
this subset of entries, kind of looking at this difference and this difference

00:16:44.970 --> 00:16:49.970
and this difference, that that's enough to point you in a pretty promising direction.

00:16:49.970 --> 00:16:54.970
But sometimes filling out this whole table can give you additional insight.

00:16:54.970 --> 00:17:01.970
Finally, we've previously talked a lot about ideas for addressing bias,

00:17:01.970 --> 00:17:05.970
talked a lot about techniques for addressing variance, but how do you address

00:17:05.970 --> 00:17:10.970
data mismatch? In particular, training on data that comes from a different

00:17:10.970 --> 00:17:15.970
distribution that your dev and test set can get you more data and really help you

00:17:15.970 --> 00:17:19.970
learn your algorithm's performance. But rather than just bias and variance problems,

00:17:19.970 --> 00:17:23.970
you now have this new potential problem of data mismatch.

00:17:23.970 --> 00:17:27.970
What are some good ways that you could use to address data mismatch?

00:17:27.970 --> 00:17:30.970
I'll be honest and say they're actually on to great or at least not very

00:17:30.970 --> 00:17:34.970
systematic ways to address data mismatch, but there are some things you could try

00:17:34.970 --> 00:17:37.970
that could help. Let's take a look at them in the next video.

00:17:37.970 --> 00:17:42.970
So what we've seen is that by using training data that can come from a different

00:17:42.970 --> 00:17:46.970
distribution as a dev and test set, this could give you a lot more data and

00:17:46.970 --> 00:17:49.970
therefore help the performance of your learning algorithm.

00:17:49.970 --> 00:17:54.970
But instead of just having bias and variance as two potential problems,

00:17:54.970 --> 00:17:57.970
you now have this third potential problem, data mismatch.

00:17:57.970 --> 00:18:01.970
So what if you perform error analysis and conclude that data mismatch is a

00:18:01.970 --> 00:18:04.970
huge source of error? How can you go about addressing that?

00:18:04.970 --> 00:18:09.970
It turns out that unfortunately there aren't super systematic ways to address

00:18:09.970 --> 00:18:13.970
data mismatch, but there are a few things you can try that could help.

